cuems-power-bridge (0.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * cluster power-off: the sequence's two stage bodies and its node selection
    move into cuemspowerbridge.cluster_shutdown, shared by POST /shutdown and
    by cuems-common's ExecStop hook, so the wall switch and the API cannot
    disagree about a cluster. The relay and the local poweroff stay with
    /shutdown: the hook must do neither, and /shutdown ends by triggering the
    transition that runs the hook, so a shared sequence would arm the relay
    twice per shutdown. The hook reaches this package as
    `python -m cuemspowerbridge.scripts.cluster_poweroff` — a module, not a
    PATH command — which keeps cluster-poweroff.conf's interpreter override and
    its missing-bridge guard working, and means no second operator command to
    power a venue down was created. 221 lines of this package's Python leave
    cuems-common's shell script, where no test could reach them.
  * shutdown: one flock on /run/cuems-power-bridge/shutdown.lock now covers a
    whole power-off sequence across processes — the daemon's asyncio lock could
    not see the ExecStop helper, nor it the daemon. The wrapper holds it across
    both stages; the daemon takes it too and RELEASES it before the local
    poweroff, because that command re-enters the same sequence through systemd
    and a still-held lock would make the wrapper run neither stage. A new
    tmpfiles rule creates the directory: /run is root-owned, so the daemon
    (User=cuems) could not create it itself.
  * shutdown: the display stage now honours projector_power_off_on_shutdown,
    skips a fleet that is already off or cooling, and skips one where no device
    answers — three gates the ExecStop path has always had and this daemon
    never did. Each can only reduce what is commanded.
  * shutdown: node self-exclusion moves into the shared stage, so the daemon
    inherits the address and hostname tests it never had. They exist to catch a
    second map entry describing this host under a different uuid; the address
    test is exact set membership, never a substring.
  * ssh: both initiators now share one host-key store,
    /var/lib/cuems/.ssh/known_hosts, pinned with -o UserKnownHostsFile. Without
    it the daemon (as cuems) and the transition (as root) kept separate stores,
    so a re-imaged node could be trusted by one and unknown to the other —
    discovered mid-shutdown. NB this re-learns the fleet once under accept-new.
  * new cuems-power-bridge-config --get <key>: a read-only query so
    cuems-common's shell scripts need none of this package's Python. The key is
    the argument, never the value, so a token never reaches argv.

  * network_map: the private ElementTree reader is REPLACED by cuemsutils'
    public configuration path. The old reader filtered nodes on the string
    "NodeType.slave"; cuems-utils feature 007 renamed that element to
    <node_role> and cuems-common's postinst converts every installed map, so
    on every upgraded controller the filter matched NOTHING. Both features
    that read the topology came back empty and said nothing: orderly
    power-off selected zero nodes, skipped the "are they down?" poll, armed
    the Shelly and cut mains on running nodes; and boot auto-load took its
    single-controller path and loaded the show before the node engines
    joined. Neither raised, warned, or failed a test — the suite was green
    because its fixtures were written in the retired vocabulary.
  * shutdown: an empty node selection is no longer indistinguishable from
    success. Six outcomes, all documented in README: a controller-only map
    proceeds; adopted nodes are targeted by default; 409 no_adopted_nodes
    when the map lists nodes but none is adopted; 409 no_resolvable_nodes
    when none can be addressed; 503 topology_unreadable when the map cannot
    be read; and a partial selection proceeds but is logged at ERROR and
    marked partial in /status. force=1 now also powers off unadopted nodes,
    and still cannot override either refusal that rests on evidence.
  * shutdown: the reachability poll no longer skips itself when the target
    list is empty. The one case where the selection was wrong was also the
    case that skipped the check which would have caught it.
  * auto-load: waits for adopted nodes only, and enters the single-controller
    settle path only after a SUCCESSFUL read. A missing or unreadable map
    previously produced an empty list that was indistinguishable from a
    genuine standalone cluster — the old reader swallowed FileNotFoundError
    and returned [].
  * status: new node_selection object (mode, found, adopted, targeted,
    partial, skipped, read_ok, read_error) so a monitor can see all of this
    without triggering a shutdown. No existing key, token or code changed
    meaning.
  * packaging: cuemsutils becomes a real, non-optional, upper-bounded
    dependency (>=0.1.0rc16,<0.1.1) in pyproject.toml and debian/control;
    Breaks: cuems-common (<< 1.3.0-23), whose cuems-cluster-poweroff carries
    the other half of this cutover. The pair upgrades together or dpkg
    refuses, instead of failing part-way through a poweroff transaction.

 -- Ion Reguera <ion@stagelab.coop>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 09:02:39 +0000

cuems-power-bridge (0.3.0-6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * auto-load: apply the node-settle margin on a SINGLE-CONTROLLER cluster.
    The settle lived inside the "wait for remote node-engines on the NNG hub"
    branch, entered only when network_map.xml lists a slave with an <ip>. A
    standalone controller (controller and node on one box) therefore skipped it
    and sent project_ready within seconds of bridge start, before its own LOCAL
    node-engine had registered its players: the project loaded but never armed,
    and arming fell to auto_load_armed_timeout_s alone. cluster_bus cannot cover
    this by design — it strips loopback and the controller's own IP, because its
    signal is deliberately "remote node peers on the hub".
  * The settle is now keyed to auto_load_wait_nodes rather than to the presence
    of remote nodes, so it applies after bus-connect on a multi-node cluster and
    on its own for a single-controller one. auto_load_wait_nodes=false still
    skips it, so an operator who disabled the node wait gets no added latency.
  * Measured at Castillo Medina del Campo (cupula1, standalone): project_ready
    at +3 s, players registered at +4 s, armed only via the 125 s backstop —
    2 min 08 after boot. Cutting the backstop to 30 s was a stopgap; this
    removes the need for the first attempt to fail at all. Multi-node clusters
    were never affected, which is why it went unnoticed: there the bus wait
    supplied the grace period as a side effect.
  * Three regression tests pin it: the settle happens with no slave in the map,
    is skipped when auto_load_wait_nodes=false, and is still applied after the
    bus wait on a multi-node cluster (that one monkeypatches cluster_bus rather
    than assigning to it, so a fake bus-wait cannot leak into later tests).

